WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:01.830
AASTHA BHARDWAJ: Welcome.

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:06.030
My name is Aastha, and I'm an
engineer on the Cloud SQL team.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:10.460
In this video, I'll show you
how to create, run, and manage

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:13.930
SQL instances using
Google Cloud SQL.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:17.450
Cloud SQL helps manage
mundane administrative tasks

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:20.390
for MySQL and Postgres
databases so you

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.190
can focus on what matters.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:27.440
Let's walk through how to create
a MySQL database on Cloud SQL

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.170
and connect to it from
the Google Cloud shell.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.240
You can set up an instance
in just a few steps.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.970
Let's go through
the basics, and I'll

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.550
point out some options for
additional configuration

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.000
along the way.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.190
We'll start by navigating to
Cloud SQL in the GCP console

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:46.480
and clicking Create Instance.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.270
And then next, choose a second
generation MySQL database

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:52.290
engine.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.380
Second generation instances
provide better performance

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.290
and improve management
tools, like automatic storage

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.930
increase.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.390
I'll name the instance
and then set the password

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:03.620
for our default account.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.860
This instance will
be created in the US,

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.930
and we'll leave
the zone set to Any

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:15.940
so Cloud SQL can choose for us.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:19.020
Let's take a look at
configuration options.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.800
You can specify MySQL
version, instant size,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.640
high availability, and more.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.640
For this demo, I'll
choose a four core,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.610
high-memory instance.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.850
Maximizing memory is a
good choice for MySQL

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.130
because it can take
advantage of the extra memory

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.720
for performance.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.020
That said, don't worry too
much about initial sizing.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.850
You can scale the
instance up or down later.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.990
Let's increase the storage
capacity to 200 gigabytes,

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.370
because increasing disk capacity
also increases performance.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.130
Check out the performance
calculator below.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.590
As I add capacity,
my disk performance

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.840
increases, which will
allow my database

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.960
to process more
operations per second.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.880
And I'll enable
Automatic Storage

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.070
Increase so that I
don't accidentally

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.570
run out of disk space.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:17.080
Lastly, let's ensure that the
database will automatically

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.480
provide isolation from failures.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.090
We can do that by enabling
the high availability

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:26.020
configuration, which, for
MySQL, adds a failover

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.550
replica in another zone.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:34.070
Now that we're happy with the
configuration of our instance,

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.310
let's kick off the creation.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.370
It may take a few minutes
for your instance to create.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:42.550
This is a great time to
grab a quick cup of coffee.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.160
You can also check the
status of your instance

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.160
by navigating to
your Instance List.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.960
Once you see a green check
mark, your instance is ready.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.660
Looks like our instance
has finished creating.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.340
Let's open the Details
page to continue.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.150
There are many ways to
connect to this instance.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:05.140
Within GCP, for example, we
could use App Engine, Compute

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.450
Engine, or Container Engine.

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:10.270
But we could also connect
from just about anywhere

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:15.570
by authorizing IP addresses
or using the Cloud SQL proxy.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:19.540
For this demo, I'll connect
using the Google Cloud Shell.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.870
Cloud Shell provides
command line access

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:26.040
to all of your Cloud resources,
such as Cloud SQL instances

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.840
directly from the browser.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.350
To open the Cloud Shell, click
Connect Using Cloud Shell.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.630
As you can see, the
relevant Gcloud command

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.820
to connect to your
Cloud SQL instance

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.720
has been pre-typed for us.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.650
So all we have to
do is press Enter.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:50.040
And then, enter our root
password from before.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.430
And now, we're connected.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:55.620
So let's create and
insert some data.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.720
To begin, I'll
create a database.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.790
Next, I'll insert some data.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:22.390
Finally, I'll make sure the
data is now in our database.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:27.930
And it is.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.800
Great.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.680
So to recap, in the
last few minutes,

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:35.290
we just created a
Cloud SQL instance

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:38.230
with high performance
and high availability,

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.380
and connected to it
with Cloud Shell.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.860
Now that you've seen how to
get started, give it a try.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:48.670
You can learn more about Google
Cloud SQL in our free trial

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:49.960
program here.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.080
Thank you for watching.

